# Where the tarball beside this file came from (#98). Update all three lines together on a bump,
# then re-run `make seed`.
#
# BUMP AT A MILESTONE, NOT AT EVERY TAG (ADR-0005). The app releases on its own cadence; gestion
# pins the version it decided to ship, so this file moves when a gestion release wants it to, not
# when the app tags.
#
# THIS ONE IS BUILT, NOT DOWNLOADED — it is our own app (ADR-0003), so there is no upstream release
# to fetch. `url=` names the tag it was built from rather than a download link, and the build is one
# command, run from the repo root:
#
#     git -C apps/farmacia archive --format=tar --prefix=farmacia/ v0.11.1 \
#       | gzip -n9 > provisioning/apps/farmacia/farmacia-0.11.1.tar.gz
#
# `--prefix=farmacia/` because ensure_vendored_app unpacks into custom_apps/ and Nextcloud
# needs the app id as the top directory — the same layout every other tarball here has. `gzip -n`
# keeps the mtime out of the header, so rebuilding the same tag yields the same bytes and the
# sha256 below stays checkable.
#
# TAG AFTER THE REBUILD, not before. Farmacia's webpack bundle bakes no version into itself, but a
# release commit whose committed js/ is not what `npm run build` produces ships a bundle that lies
# about the tree it came from. The app's own gates catch that before tagging — MetadataTest's
# version agreement and the zero-diff `git status --porcelain js/` drift check after a rebuild,
# both under `make test` in the app repo. v0.11.1 was cut with them green.
#
# `git archive` exports the TRACKED tree, which is why node_modules/, vendor/ and tools/ are not
# in here: all three are gitignored in the app's repo. Result is 469 KB over 101 files.
#
# ONE tarball in this directory, ever: ensure_vendored_app globs *.tar.gz and fails on anything
# other than exactly one. A bump deletes the previous file, it does not sit beside it.

